    "number": "Theorem (thm:lbmainthm)",
    "quote": "Let $\\mathcal{L}$ be an algorithm that solves \\QFK. Then, there exists an \ninstance $(\\A, n, m, k, \\epsilon, \\delta)$, \nwith $0< \\epsilon \\leq \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{32}}$, $0 < \\delta \\leq \\frac{e^{-1}}{4}$, \nand $n \\geq 2m$, $1 \\leq k \\leq m$, on which the expected number of pulls \nperformed by $\\mathcal{L}$ is at least $\\frac{1}{18375}. \\frac{1}{\\epsilon^2}. \\frac{n}{m-k+1}\\ln\\frac{\\binom{m}{k - 1}}{4\\delta}$."
   },
   "confidence": "high",
   "notes": "n is K. Algorithm class: algorithms that solve (k,m,n) for every instance (delta-PAC)."
